{
 "format": "bms-flow-1",
 "title": "The hypothalamic–pituitary–adrenal axis",
 "description": "Stress makes the hypothalamus release corticotropin-releasing hormone (CRH) into the portal vessels; CRH makes the corticotrophs of the anterior pituitary release ACTH; ACTH makes the zona fasciculata of the adrenal cortex release cortisol. Cortisol feeds back on both the hypothalamus and the pituitary (long-loop negative feedback). The levels are relative to their resting levels (1). Run it, then knock out the adrenal cortex (as in Addison’s disease), or fix cortisol at a high level (as in a patient taking a glucocorticoid drug), and run it again.",
